data: {"run_id": "20260929-024545-64d2f9", "report": {"title": "How Large Language Models Relate to Neural Networks", "summary_claims": [{"text": "An LLM is a type of neural network that specifically uses transformer architectures.", "kind": "factual", "answer_slot": "relationship", "evidence_ids": ["S5-e3"], "citation_ids": ["S5"]}], "key_findings": [], "sections": [{"heading": "Tasks and architecture", "claims": [{"text": "Transformers are described as the most common architecture for LLMs.", "kind": "factual", "answer_slot": "dimension", "evidence_ids": ["S5-e4"], "citation_ids": ["S5"]}]}], "contradictions": [], "limitations": ["The evidence does not establish a general comparison of LLMs and neural networks across training objectives or scale; it gives only limited information about how LLM training differs from neural-network training more broadly.", "The evidence describes text training and language tasks for LLMs, but does not establish that all LLMs use the same training data, objectives, or task set.", "Only limited evidence was found for: How do LLMs differ from neural networks more generally in training data, objectives, scale, and intended tasks.", "Only limited evidence was found for: What common misconceptions obscure the distinction between LLMs and neural networks\u2014for example, whether every neural network is an LLM or whether all LLMs use the same design.", "1 extracted finding(s) were excluded because their quotes could not be located in the source text", "4 generated claim(s) were excluded because the cited evidence did not support them, or because verification did not reach them within this run's budget.", "The evidence did not establish an explicit statement of how the subjects differ."]}, "plan": {"sub_questions": [{"id": "SQ1", "text": "What is a neural network, and what defines a large language model (LLM)?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ2", "text": "Are LLMs neural networks, and how does the category relationship work between the broad class of neural networks and the narrower class of LLMs?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ3", "text": "What neural-network architectures do modern LLMs typically use, and how do those architectures support language processing and generation?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ4", "text": "How do LLMs differ from neural networks more generally in training data, objectives, scale, and intended tasks?", "is_followup": false}, {"id": "SQ5", "text": "What common misconceptions obscure the distinction between LLMs and neural networks\u2014for example, whether every neural network is an LLM or whether all LLMs use the same design?", "is_followup": false}]}, "evidence": [{"id": "S1-e1", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "An LLM is typically a neural-network AI model trained on a large amount of text for natural-language tasks, especially language generation.", "quote": "A large language model (LLM) is an AI model (typically a neural network \"Neural network (machine learning)\")) trained on a vast amount of text for natural language processing tasks, especially language generation.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e2", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "The source places LLMs within the broader category of AI models and says they are typically neural networks; it does not say that every neural network is an LLM.", "quote": "A large language model (LLM) is an AI model (typically a neural network \"Neural network (machine learning)\")) trained on a vast amount of text for natural language processing tasks, especially language generation.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.94, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q3", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S1-e3", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "LLMs typically use transformer architecture, while the source describes encoder-only and decoder-only transformer model configurations.", "quote": "LLMs are typically based on transformer \"Transformer (deep learning)\") architecture.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.94, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e4", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "The source describes distinct transformer configurations, including encoder-only BERT and decoder-only GPT models.", "quote": "Though the original transformer has both encoder and decoder blocks, BERT is an encoder-only model. Academic and research usage of BERT began to decline in 2023, following rapid improvements in the abilities of decoder-only models (such as GPT) to solve tasks via prompting.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.86, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e5", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "The source characterizes LLMs by their large text training data and language-oriented tasks, and says GPTs are pretrained for next-word prediction and often fine-tuned for assistant behavior.", "quote": "Generative pre-trained transformers (GPTs) are a type of LLM that is pre-trained to predict the next word. GPTs are then often fine-tuned \"Fine-tuning (deep learning)\") to follow instructions and to behave as assistants.", "quote_match": "none", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.141, "citable": false, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S1-e6", "source_id": "S1", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "The source describes different transformer model configurations rather than a single uniform design.", "quote": "Though the original transformer has both encoder and decoder blocks, BERT is an encoder-only model. Academic and research usage of BERT began to decline in 2023, following rapid improvements in the abilities of decoder-only models (such as GPT) to solve tasks via prompting.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.75, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S2-e1", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Transformer LLMs may use encoder-decoder or decoder-only designs, with encoder-decoder models suited to translation and decoder-only models suited to generation.", "quote": "An Encoder-Decoder model (like T5) is ideal for translation. A Decoder-only model (like GPT) excels at generation \u2014 from code to conversation.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S2-e2", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Many modern LLMs based on Transformer Decoders generate sequentially by predicting one token at a time from prior tokens, with causal masking blocking access to future tokens.", "quote": "This step-by-step, sequential generation is a defining characteristic of many modern LLMs, particularly those based on the Transformer Decoder. This \u201cleft-to-right\u201d flow is achieved and enforced by:", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.99, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S2-e3", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Causal masking prevents a Transformer Decoder from using future tokens when predicting the current token.", "quote": "This masking prevents the model\u2019s attention mechanism from \u201cseeing\u201d or using information from future tokens when predicting the current one.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.97, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S2-e4", "source_id": "S2", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "The article describes next-token prediction as the objective repeated during autoregressive generation.", "quote": "The Autoregressive Flow we discussed happens as the Decoder Blocks process the sequence (including previously generated tokens) and the LM Head makes the next token prediction, repeating this cycle until the output is complete.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.75, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e1", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "An LLM is a deep-learning model for a broad range of natural-language tasks and is a type of neural network using transformer architecture.", "quote": "At its core, a large language model (LLM) is a model trained using deep learning algorithms and capable of a broad range of natural language processing (NLP) tasks, such as sentiment analysis, conversational question answering, text translation, classification, and generation.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e2", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "The source describes neural networks as layers of processing units often compared to neurons.", "quote": "These neural networks are made up of layers of processing units, often compared to neurons in the brain.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.9, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e3", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "The source presents LLMs as a specialized subtype of neural networks, not as a category separate from neural networks.", "quote": "An LLM is a type of neural network (NN) that specifically uses transformer architectures, which are models designed to detect dependencies between different parts of a sequence of data, regardless of their distance from each other.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q3", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S5-e4", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Transformers are the most common LLM architecture, but some use an encoder-decoder configuration while others, such as GPT, use only a decoder.", "quote": "A transformer model is the most common architecture of a large language model. It typically consists of both an encoder and a decoder, though some models, like GPT, use only the decoder.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e5", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Self-attention helps transformer models capture relationships between distant words and process information in parallel.", "quote": "Transformer models work with self-attention mechanisms, which enable the models to learn more quickly than traditional models like long short-term memory models. Self-attention is what enables transformer models to capture relationships between words, even those far apart in a sentence, better than older models, primarily by allowing parallel processing of information.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S5-e6", "source_id": "S5", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "LLMs are pretrained on very large textual datasets, whose quality can affect model performance.", "quote": "Large language models are pretrained using large textual datasets from sites like Wikipedia and GitHub. These datasets consist of trillions of words, and their quality is set to affect the language model's performance.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.95, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e1", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Encoder-style transformers use self-attention to encode tokens and learn embeddings for predictive tasks, while decoder-style transformers use self-attention to generate text.", "quote": "Fundamentally, both encoder- and decoder-style architectures use the same self-attention layers to encode word tokens. However, the main difference is that encoders are designed to learn embeddings that can be used for various predictive modeling tasks such as classification. In contrast, decoders are designed to generate new texts, for example, answering user queries.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.99, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S6-e2", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Decoder self-attention is masked so generation is autoregressive: each next token uses previously generated tokens as context.", "quote": "The autoregressive property ensures that the model generates output tokens one at a time and uses previously generated tokens as context for generating the next word token.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S6-e3", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Encoder-decoder models are suited to tasks that require understanding an input sequence and generating a related output, including translation and summarization.", "quote": "Encoder-decoder models are typically used for natural language processing tasks that involve understanding input sequences and generating output sequences, often with different lengths and structures. They are particularly good at tasks where there is a complex mapping between the input and output sequences and where it is crucial to capture the relationships between the elements in both sequences.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q5", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S6-e4", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "The source describes GPT models as decoder-only models trained on large-scale unsupervised text and fine-tuned for specific tasks.", "quote": "The GPT (Generative Pre-trained Transformer) series are decoder-only models pretrained on large-scale unsupervised text data and finetuned for specific tasks such as text classification, sentiment analysis, question-answering, and summarization.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.99, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e5", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "BERT is pretrained on a large text corpus using masked language modeling and next-sentence prediction objectives.", "quote": "BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers) is an encoder-only architecture based on the Transformer's encoder module. The BERT model is pretrained on a large text corpus using masked language modeling (illustrated in the figure below) and next-sentence prediction tasks.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.97, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S6-e6", "source_id": "S6", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "The source describes multiple transformer architecture types, rather than a single design shared by all models.", "quote": "Since the first transformer architecture emerged, hundreds of encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder hybrids have been developed, as summarized in the figure below.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.95, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e1", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "Neural networks include architectures that serve as fundamental building blocks of AI and machine learning.", "quote": "These architectures, such as Feedforward Networks, Convolutional Neural Networks, and Recurrent Neural Networks, form the fundamental building blocks of AI and machine learning.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.78, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e2", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ1", "claim": "The source characterizes LLMs as deep-learning models built on neural-network architectures.", "quote": "Large Language Models (LLMs) are a specific class of deep learning models built upon neural network architectures.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.95, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e3", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ2", "claim": "LLMs are a narrower class within the broader category of neural networks.", "quote": "Large Language Models (LLMs) are a specific class of deep learning models built upon neural network architectures.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 1.0, "citable": true, "query_id": "Q4", "cross_attributed": false}, {"id": "S3-e4", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ3", "claim": "Modern LLMs predominantly use Transformers, whose attention mechanisms enable parallel sequence processing and improved capture of contextual relationships.", "quote": "The Transformer\u2019s unique reliance on attention mechanisms, which allow for parallel processing of sequences and a significant improvement in capturing contextual relationships, outperformed previous sequential models in many natural language tasks.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.98, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e5", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ4", "claim": "LLMs are trained on vast quantities of text to learn patterns and relationships in human language.", "quote": "These models are trained on vast quantities of text data, allowing them to learn intricate patterns and relationships within human language.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.95, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}, {"id": "S3-e6", "source_id": "S3", "sub_question_id": "SQ5", "claim": "The source indicates that LLMs do not all use an identical design, since advanced models continue to incorporate architectural innovations beyond the central Transformer architecture.", "quote": "While the Transformer is central, the most advanced LLMs continue incorporating further architectural innovations and training techniques.", "quote_match": "exact_normalized", "page": null, "stance": "supports", "confidence": 0.91, "citable": true, "query_id": "", "cross_attributed": true}], "sources": [{"id": "S1", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model", "title": "Large language model - Wikipedia", "domain": "en.wikipedia.org", "source_type": "other", "content_origin": "provider_raw", "quality_score": 0.619, "page_count": null, "usable": true, "fetch_status": "provider_content"}, {"id": "S2", "url": "https://blog.gopenai.com/anatomy-of-llms-transformer-overview-encoder-decoder-autoregressive-flow-6a59bc839710", "title": "Anatomy of LLMs: Transformer Overview \u2014 Encoder, Decoder & Autoregressive Flow", "domain": "blog.gopenai.com", "source_type": "other", "content_origin": "provider_raw", "quality_score": 0.615, "page_count": null, "usable": true, "fetch_status": "provider_content"}, {"id": "S3", "url": "https://medium.com/ai-simplified-in-plain-english/the-architectural-evolution-of-neural-networks-in-large-language-models-5846978d2fac", "title": "The Architectural Evolution of Neural Networks in Large Language Models", "domain": "medium.com", "source_type": "blog", "content_or