pecifically uses transformer architectures. [S5]\n\n## Tasks and architecture\n\nTransformers are described as the most common architecture for LLMs. [S5]\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The evidence does not establish a general comparison of LLMs and neural networks across training objectives or scale; it gives only limited information about how LLM training differs from neural-network training more broadly.\n- The evidence describes text training and language tasks for LLMs, but does not establish that all LLMs use the same training data, objectives, or task set.\n- Only limited evidence was found for: How do LLMs differ from neural networks more generally in training data, objectives, scale, and intended tasks.\n- Only limited evidence was found for: What common misconceptions obscure the distinction between LLMs and neural networks\u2014for example, whether every neural network is an LLM or whether all LLMs use the same design.\n- 1 extracted finding(s) were excluded because their quotes could not be located in the source text\n- 4 generated claim(s) were excluded because the cited evidence did not support them, or because verification did not reach them within this run's budget.\n- The evidence did not establish an explicit statement of how the subjects differ.\n\n## Sources\n\n- **[S1]** [Large language model - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model) \u2014 en.wikipedia.org, other, n.d., quality 0.62 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S2]** [Anatomy of LLMs: Transformer Overview \u2014 Encoder, Decoder & Autoregressive Flow](https://blog.gopenai.com/anatomy-of-llms-transformer-overview-encoder-decoder-autoregressive-flow-6a59bc839710) \u2014 blog.gopenai.com, other, n.d., quality 0.61 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S3]** [The Architectural Evolution of Neural Networks in Large Language Models](https://medium.com/ai-simplified-in-plain-english/the-architectural-evolution-of-neural-networks-in-large-language-models-5846978d2fac) \u2014 medium.com, blog, n.d., quality 0.56 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n- **[S5]** [Understanding large language models: A comprehensive guide | Elastic](https://www.elastic.co/what-is/large-language-models) \u2014 elastic.co, other, n.d., quality 0.60\n- **[S6]** [Understanding Encoder And Decoder LLMs](https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/p/understanding-encoder-and-decoder) \u2014 magazine.sebastianraschka.com, other, n.d., quality 0.60 _(retrieved, not cited)_\n\n## Citation verification\n\n- Evidence references: 2, 2 resolved to citable evidence (100%). Citation markers are derived from those references by the engine, so citation integrity is a structural invariant rather than a measurement.\n- Evidence-owing claims carrying a citation: 100% of 2\n- Entailment checked against each claim's own evidence, over every eligible claim: 2 supported, 1 partially supported, 3 unsupported, 0 not checked\n- Retrieved but never cited: S1, S2, S3, S6\n\n<details><summary>Open citation issues</summary>\n\n- `unsupported_claim` best entailment 0.007 from S3-e1 is below the 0.98 support threshold \u2014 The evidence distinguishes LLMs as a specific class built on neural-network architectures from the broader set of neural\n- `partially_supported_claim` best entailment 0.851 from S3-e5 is below the 0.98 support threshold \u2014 LLMs are trained on vast quantities of text data to learn patterns and relationships in human language.\n- `unsupported_claim` does not answer the question: the claim describes one subject rather than contrasting them, so it cannot fill the contrast slot \u2014 Transformer models include encoder-only, decoder-only, and encoder-decoder hybrid designs.\n- `unsupported_claim` does not answer the question: It describes tasks LLMs can perform, not how they relate to or differ from neural networks. \u2014 LLMs are described as capable of a broad range of natural-language tasks, including sentiment analysis, conversational q\n\n</details>\n\n## Run metrics\n\n| Metric | Value |\n| --- 